{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.9,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["C12"],
      "pins": ["C12.1", "C12.2"],
      "nets": ["BAT+", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the EthernetAndPower sheet the input decoupling capacitor C12, connected between the BAT+ rail and GND at the FAN53610 input, is drawn with the value '2.2nF' (note the 'n' prefix, distinct from the 'u' used on sibling caps C13=10uF and C11=0.1uF).",
      "expected": "The specification states C12 is the 2.2 µF input capacitor (CIN) of the FAN53610AUC33X buck regulator, matching the datasheet's recommended 2.2 µF CIN. A 2.2 nF value is 1000× too small for this position.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "EthernetAndPower.kicad_sch: 'C12 2.2nF' tied from BAT+ to GND at IC1 input"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "'C12 (2.2 µF) on BAT+→GND is the input cap; C13 (10 µF) on +3V3→GND is the output cap — these exactly match the FAN53610 datasheet's recommended 2.2 µF CIN / 10 µF COUT values'"}
      ],
      "impact": "With only 2.2 nF of input capacitance, the 1 A synchronous buck has effectively no high-frequency input bypass. The BAT+ rail will exhibit large switching ripple, the regulator may become unstable or fail to start/hold regulation under load, EMI will increase, and the +3V3 rail (feeding the SKW92A, keyboard, microSD and EPD switch) is at risk of brown-out.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic is largely consistent with the specification, but the FAN53610 input decoupling capacitor C12 is drawn as 2.2 nF instead of the specified/datasheet-recommended 2.2 µF, a 1000× value error on a power-rail bypass that would compromise regulator stability and the 3.3 V rail. No other deviation is supported by the drawing with comparable confidence."
}

